#Problem statement: To Configure Nginx and PHP-FPM 8.3 Using UNIX Sockets
    1. install and configure Nginx to serve traffic on a custom port (e.g., 8096) using a specific document root (e.g., /var/www/html)
    2. install PHP-FPM (specifically version 8.3) and configure it to use a UNIX socket located at /var/run/php-fpm/default.sock
    3. bridge the gap so Nginx successfully passes PHP requests to our new PHP-FPM socket
    4. verify the entire setup is working from a remote jump host

Steps:
1. Prepare the Server. Before configuring services, you need to access your target application server and ensure you have the right permissions.

        SSH into your target application server.

        Elevate your privileges to root so you can install packages and modify system configurations:

            sudo su -
2. Install Nginx and PHP-FPM 8.3. First, let's tackle the package installations. Depending on your Linux distribution (this guide assumes an RHEL/CentOS-based system like Rocky or AlmaLinux), you might need to enable specific module streams for newer PHP versions.
            yum install nginx -y
3. Install PHP-FPM 8.3: Enable the PHP 8.3 module and install the FPM package:                        
            dnf module enable php:8.3 -y
            yum install php-fpm -y
4. Configure PHP-FPM for UNIX Sockets. By default, PHP-FPM often listens on a TCP port (like 127.0.0.1:9000). UNIX sockets are generally faster for inter-process communication on the same server because they bypass the network stack overhead.

    Create the socket directory: Ensure the parent directory exists.
            mkdir -p /var/run/php-fpm
5. Edit the Pool Configuration: Open the default FPM pool file, typically found at /etc/php-fpm.d/www.conf
6. Update the Listen Directive: Find the listen parameter and point it to your new socket:
            listen = /var/run/php-fpm/default.sock
7. Set Permissions: Nginx needs permission to read and write to this socket. In the same www.conf file, update the ownership parameters:
            listen.owner = nginx
            listen.group = nginx
            listen.mode = 0660
            user = nginx
            group = nginx                        
8. Start and Enable the Service:
            systemctl start php-fpm
            systemctl enable php-fpm            
9. Configure Nginx Server Blocks. Now we need to tell Nginx how to handle web traffic and where to send PHP files.            
    Edit the Nginx Config: Open /etc/nginx/nginx.conf.

    Modify the Server Block: Update the default server block to listen on your custom port (8096), define the root directory, and add a location block for PHP processing.
        server {
            listen       8096;
            listen       [::]:8096;
            server_name  _;
            root         /var/www/html;
            index        index.php index.html index.htm;

            location / {
                try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
            }

        # Pass PHP scripts to FastCGI server listening on the UNIX socket
            location ~ \.php$ {
            fastcgi_pass   unix:/var/run/php-fpm/default.sock;
            fastcgi_index  index.php;
            fastcgi_param  SCRIPT_FILENAME $document_root$fastcgi_script_name;
            include        fastcgi_params;
            }
        }
10. Test and Start Nginx: Always test your syntax before restarting a web server!
        nginx -t
        systemctl start nginx
        systemctl enable nginx

11. Verify the Setup. The final requirement is to test the configuration from an external machine (like a jump host). Assuming you have an index.php file in /var/www/html, exit out of your application server back to your jump host and run a simple curl command:        
        curl http://<your_server_ip_or_hostname>:8096/index.php

12. If the terminal returns the parsed HTML/PHP output without throwing a 502 Bad Gateway or 404 Not Found error, congratulations! You have successfully deployed a robust Nginx and PHP-FPM stack.

13. Conclusion
Mastering the interaction between Nginx and PHP-FPM via UNIX sockets is a fundamental skill for any DevOps engineer. It not only improves application performance by reducing network overhead but also gives you finer control over process management and security permissions.

Happy deploying!